<p>Minoxidil and finasteride are the two most established medicines for pattern hair loss, and both are prescription medicines in India. Each appears in Schedule H of the Drugs Rules, 1945, which means a pharmacy should sell them only against a prescription from a registered medical practitioner. Both work only for as long as you keep using them, and both have side effects that deserve a real conversation.</p>

<p>That last point is the one most often skipped. Subscription kits, online questionnaires and pharmacy counters make these medicines feel like grooming products. They are not. Finasteride in particular has sexual and mood-related side effects that are uncommon but real, and specific precautions around pregnancy. None of that makes these medicines dangerous for most people who take them. It means the decision should be made knowingly, after a diagnosis, with a doctor accountable for it.</p>

<h2 id="what-minoxidil-does">What minoxidil does</h2>

<p>Minoxidil was first developed as a blood pressure tablet, and increased hair growth was noticed as a side effect. Applied to the scalp, it appears to prolong the growth phase of hair follicles and enlarge miniaturised ones, although its exact mechanism in hair is still not fully understood. It does not act on hormones.</p>

<p>Topical minoxidil is used for pattern hair loss in both men and women. <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/minoxidil-solution" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's page on minoxidil solution</a> notes that results take months to appear, that regrown hair can be finer than the original, and that if treatment stops, the scalp generally returns to its pre-treatment appearance within three or four months.</p>

<h3>Oral minoxidil</h3>

<p>Low-dose oral minoxidil is increasingly prescribed for hair loss, but its use for this purpose is off-label, meaning outside the use it was originally licensed for. DermNet notes that it can increase scalp hair growth but may also cause generalised excess hair growth and other adverse effects. Because the tablet is a blood pressure medicine, it needs a medical history, attention to heart health and blood pressure, and follow-up. It is not something to start from a relative's leftover strip or a product bundle.</p>

<h2 id="what-finasteride-does">What finasteride does</h2>

<p>Finasteride blocks the enzyme 5-alpha reductase, which converts testosterone into dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT drives the miniaturisation of genetically susceptible scalp follicles in male pattern hair loss, the process mapped by <a href="/blog/norwood-scale-explained">the Norwood scale</a>. Lowering DHT slows that process, and in many men it stabilises the hair and allows some recovery in thinning areas.</p>

<p>It is used for male pattern hair loss. <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/finasteride" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's finasteride page</a> notes that it is not indicated for women with pattern hair loss, though it is occasionally used off-label after menopause. Like minoxidil, it takes months to show an effect, and the benefit is lost gradually after stopping, because the underlying process resumes.</p>

<h2 id="prescription-rules-in-india">The prescription rules in India</h2>

<p>Both medicines are listed in Schedule H of the <a href="https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2022/drug_rules/Drugs%20Rules%201945_2024%2009.pdf" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">Drugs Rules, 1945, as published by the Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation</a>: finasteride at entry 198 and minoxidil at entry 329. Schedule H medicines carry the Rx symbol and a warning that they are not to be sold by retail without the prescription of a registered medical practitioner. The minoxidil entry is not limited to tablets, so solutions and foams are covered too.</p>

<p>Anyone who has bought minoxidil across a counter in Gurgaon knows that enforcement is uneven. That does not change what the rule is for. A prescription requirement exists so that someone confirms the diagnosis is correct, that the medicine suits you, and that you understand what to watch for.</p>

<h3>Online prescriptions</h3>

<p>A prescription issued remotely by a registered doctor can be entirely legitimate. What matters is whether a doctor actually assessed you: your history, your other medicines, your scalp, and your reasons for wanting treatment. A form that steers every answer towards the same product bundle, with a prescription generated at the end, is a sales process with a doctor's name attached.</p>

<p>Three questions test it quickly. Were you asked about mood, sexual health, other medicines and pregnancy plans? Did anyone examine your scalp, or at least review clear photographs of it? And is there a named doctor you can reach if a side effect appears? If the answer to all three is no, the prescription was a formality.</p>

<h2 id="minoxidil-side-effects">Minoxidil side effects</h2>

<h3>Topical minoxidil</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Scalp irritation,</strong> itching and flaking, which DermNet describes as common. It is sometimes due to the propylene glycol base rather than the minoxidil itself, and a different formulation can help.</li>
  <li><strong>Allergic contact dermatitis,</strong> which is rare but needs review, particularly if redness spreads or the scalp becomes sore.</li>
  <li><strong>Unwanted facial hair,</strong> usually where the product has run onto the forehead or cheeks or transferred from a pillow. It tends to settle after stopping.</li>
  <li><strong>Temporary increased shedding</strong> in the early weeks, commonly attributed to the hair cycle shifting. It is not a sign the treatment is failing, but it is a frequent reason people give up too soon.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Oral minoxidil</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Increased hair growth on the face and body, the most commonly discussed effect.</li>
  <li>Effects on the heart and circulation, such as a faster heartbeat, light-headedness and ankle swelling from fluid retention, which is why a history and follow-up matter.</li>
  <li>Rarely, more serious cardiovascular effects, which is why it is avoided in people with certain heart conditions.</li>
</ul>

<p>DermNet advises that minoxidil should be avoided during pregnancy and breastfeeding. It is also toxic to cats and dogs, so topical products, and hands and pillowcases that have touched them, should be kept away from pets.</p>

<h2 id="finasteride-side-effects">Finasteride side effects</h2>

<p>Most men who take finasteride do not experience side effects. A minority do, and those side effects are the reason it should never be started casually.</p>

<h3>Sexual side effects</h3>

<p>Reduced libido, erectile difficulties and changes in ejaculation are the recognised sexual side effects. The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/finasteride/side-effects-of-finasteride/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS information on the side effects of finasteride</a> notes that these can sometimes carry on even after the medicine is stopped. That is uncommon, but it should be discussed before starting, not discovered afterwards.</p>

<h3>Mood changes</h3>

<p>Depression, low mood and, rarely, suicidal thoughts have been reported. The NHS lists depression as a serious side effect, advises people taking finasteride for hair loss to stop it straight away if they notice low mood, and treats thoughts of self-harm as an emergency. Anyone with a history of depression or anxiety should raise it at consultation, and it is sensible to tell someone close to you that you have started the medicine, so a change in mood is noticed early.</p>

<h3>Breast changes and PSA</h3>

<p>Breast tenderness or enlargement can occur, and any lump, pain or nipple discharge should be reported to a doctor promptly. Finasteride also lowers PSA readings, the blood test used in prostate assessment, so any doctor checking your PSA needs to know you take it.</p>

<h3>Post-finasteride syndrome</h3>

<p>"Post-finasteride syndrome" is a term used for sexual, physical or psychological symptoms that some men report persisting after they stop the drug. Its nature and cause are still debated, and it is not well understood. What is not in dispute is that persistent symptoms have been reported, and that health services such as the NHS now reflect persistent sexual side effects in their patient information. The honest position is neither to dismiss these reports nor to present them as a likely outcome. They are a reason for an informed decision, a low threshold for reporting new symptoms, and a prescriber who takes those reports seriously.</p>

<h2 id="finasteride-pregnancy-and-women">Finasteride, pregnancy and women</h2>

<p>Finasteride can affect the development of the genitals of a male fetus, and DermNet states that it is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant. Product information for finasteride also advises that women who are or may be pregnant should not handle crushed or broken tablets, because the drug can be absorbed through the skin. Intact coated tablets do not carry the same handling concern, but they should still be stored out of reach.</p>

<p>For men whose partners are pregnant or trying to conceive, NHS patient information notes that only very small amounts of finasteride reach semen and that harm to a pregnancy from this is very unlikely. Some manufacturers still suggest condoms as a precaution, which the NHS describes as unlikely to be necessary. Finasteride can occasionally affect sperm quality, which usually returns to normal after stopping, so couples trying to conceive should mention it.</p>

<p>For women with pattern hair loss, the options and precautions differ enough to need their own discussion; <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">female pattern hair loss and its workup</a> covers them.</p>

