<h2 id="subscription-boxes-and-questionnaires">Subscription boxes and online questionnaires</h2>

<p>Hair-loss kits sold online tend to follow the same shape: a short questionnaire, a recommendation, then a monthly box containing minoxidil, a finasteride prescription or both, often packaged with shampoos, supplements and serums. Some include a genuine teleconsultation. Others do not. The model has real appeal, including convenience, privacy and no waiting room, and it is worth being clear about what it cannot do.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A questionnaire is not a diagnosis.</strong> Telogen effluvium, iron deficiency, thyroid disease, alopecia areata, scalp conditions and scarring alopecias all present as hair fall. None is treated with finasteride, and some worsen if the real cause goes unaddressed for a year. <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">Telling a temporary shed apart from pattern thinning</a> requires looking at the scalp.</li>
  <li><strong>Nobody examines the scalp.</strong> Trichoscopy is what shows miniaturisation and provides a baseline for judging whether treatment is working.</li>
  <li><strong>Nobody checks the blood when it matters.</strong> <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">Which blood tests matter for hair fall</a> explains when testing changes the plan.</li>
  <li><strong>Side-effect conversations get compressed</strong> into a tick box, which is the wrong format for sexual and mood-related effects.</li>
  <li><strong>The bundle decides the treatment.</strong> When the product range is fixed, every customer tends to receive a version of the same thing, often including supplements with little evidence behind them.</li>
  <li><strong>The subscription renews regardless.</strong> A monthly delivery continues whether or not anyone has checked that the treatment is working or asked about side effects.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you already use one of these services and are doing well, you do not necessarily need to stop. The point is to make sure someone has confirmed the diagnosis and that you know which side effects to report.</p>

<h2 id="stopping-and-judging-results">What happens if you stop, and how to judge whether it is working</h2>

<p>Both medicines are maintenance treatments. Stopping does no harm beyond losing the benefit, but the benefit does go: the hair drifts back towards the course it would have followed without treatment, usually over several months. That is not a withdrawal effect; it is the underlying condition continuing. The same logic applies to <a href="/blog/does-prp-work-for-hair-loss">PRP and other adjunct treatments</a>, and it is also why <a href="/blog/is-a-hair-transplant-permanent">a hair transplant does not stop native hair loss</a>.</p>

<p>To judge whether treatment is working:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Allow enough time.</strong> Assessments are generally made after six months or more, not after six weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Use standardised photographs</strong> taken before starting and repeated with the same light, angle and hair length. <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">How to read before-and-after photographs</a> explains why casual photographs mislead.</li>
  <li><strong>Count stabilisation as a result.</strong> For many people, no further loss is the achievement.</li>
  <li><strong>Review at fixed intervals with the prescribing doctor,</strong> including a check for side effects, rather than adjusting how you take the medicine on your own.</li>
</ol>

<h3>What a consultation adds</h3>

<p>These are established prescription medicines with a defined place in treating pattern hair loss, and like any medicine they suit some people and not others. The decision to take them should be made knowingly. A proper assessment confirms that the hair loss is pattern loss, looks for anything else contributing, weighs your medical history, including mood, sexual health, heart health and pregnancy plans, and agrees when and how the result will be reviewed.</p>

<p>Whoever prescribes, the prescription should follow an examination rather than replace it; <a href="/hair-fall-treatment-in-gurgaon">how a hair fall assessment works</a> sets out what that examination involves. If you want to think through your situation first, the <a href="/hair-loss-assessment">hair loss self-assessment</a> is a sensible starting point, and <a href="/medical-disclaimer#doctors">the clinic's doctors and their registration details</a> are listed separately.</p>

